{"type":"message","id":"dd27e9f6","parentId":"0c47ebd0","timestamp":"2026-10-06T21:17:46.239Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"**Analyzing Hebrew Translation**\n\nI'm currently dissecting the first turn of the Hebrew conversation to understand its English equivalent. My aim is to accurately back-translate this multi-turn dialogue, focusing on the nuances of each statement.\n\n**Translating Scotland's Status**\n\nI'm working on pinpointing the precise English rendering for the initial Hebrew exchange concerning Scotland's independence. My focus remains on capturing the precise meaning in the back-translation.\n\n**Clarifying Referendum Details**\n\nI'm now focusing on the 2014 Scottish independence referendum. I'm exploring how close the vote was and investigating the ongoing independence efforts, considering the ultimate dependence on public will and government actions.\n\n**Detailing Referendum Figures**\n\nI'm now focused on the 2014 Scottish independence referendum results, noting the 55.3% vote to remain versus 44.7% for independence. The turnout was 84.6% with over 3.6 million valid votes cast.\n\n**Analyzing Referendum Impact**\n\nI'm now evaluating the decisiveness of the 2014 referendum, noting the relatively close vote but its sufficiency in settling independence matters for the time being. I'm also beginning to consider the next potential opportunity for such a vote.\n\n**Considering Future Votes**\n\nI am now examining the post-2014 landscape, specifically the SNP's push for a second referendum in 2017 which was blocked by the UK government. I'm also looking at the Scottish government's moves for greater devolution, and the UK's stance on needing a \"clear and sustained\" majority before any further votes.\n\n**Assessing Independence Prospects**\n\nMy current focus is on the indeterminate nature of Scotland's next independence referendum. I'm examining the SNP's 2017 proposal, its rejection by the UK government, and